A patch on upstream Mermaid at the pin, for the reference ferrigura is checked against
(docs-port/ORACLE-PLAN.md). Applied in the order of the file names by tools/reference/setup.sh.

fix(flowchart): reject inherited names as shapes and de-duplicate __proto__

isValidShape used `in`, so `constructor` passed as a shape name; it now
checks own keys. Subgraph de-duplication marked ids in a plain object, so
a node named `__proto__` was never recognised as seen; it now uses a Set.
Both were found while planning the Rust flowchart parser, which would
otherwise have needed exceptions in the differential run.

The fork's commit 764814a2d, its changes to packages/ and .build/.

diff --git a/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/flowchart/flowDb.ts b/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/flowchart/flowDb.ts
index 3b2cd013f..9f7acc7c3 100644
--- a/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/flowchart/flowDb.ts
+++ b/packages/mermaid/src/diagrams/flowchart/flowDb.ts
@@ -683,7 +683,12 @@ You have to call mermaid.initialize.`
     }
 
     const uniq = (a: any[]) => {
-      const prims: any = { boolean: {}, number: {}, string: {} };
+      // Sets, not plain objects: an id such as `__proto__` must count as an ordinary key.
+      const prims: Record<string, Set<unknown>> = {
+        boolean: new Set(),
+        number: new Set(),
+        string: new Set(),
+      };
       const objs: any[] = [];
 
       let dir: string | undefined;
@@ -698,7 +703,11 @@ You have to call mermaid.initialize.`
           return false;
         }
         if (type in prims) {
-          return prims[type].hasOwnProperty(item) ? false : (prims[type][item] = true);
+          if (prims[type].has(item)) {
+            return false;
+          }
+          prims[type].add(item);
+          return true;
         } else {
           return objs.includes(item) ? false : objs.push(item);
         }
